    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `build_context` in `app/context.py` and added tests for it. All 7 tests pass (the 4 existing ones plus 3 new ones).\n\n**How it works:**\n- It calls `retriever.invoke(question, config={\"tags\": [TRACE_TAG]})`. This is the standard entry point that every LangChain retriever has, so it works the same with `KeywordRetriever` and a vector-store retriever. The tag is attached to the lookup's trace run, so the lookup can be found in the traces by `TRACE_TAG` (`\"kb-context\"`).\n- It goes through the documents in the order the retriever returned them and skips any whose `metadata[\"source\"]` has already been used.\n- It stops after `MAX_SOURCES` entries.\n- Each entry is `[n] <source>` followed by the document text with surrounding whitespace removed. Entries are joined with one blank line, and the result is `\"\"` when nothing is found.\n- No dependencies were added.\n\n**Tests (`tests/test_context.py`):**\n- An exact output check for duplicate removal, the limit, numbering and whitespace trimming.\n- The empty-result case.\n- A check that `TRACE_TAG` reaches the retriever run. My first version of this test failed, but only because the test attached its recorder in the wrong place. The implementation didn't change, and the test now reads the tags directly from the retriever run.\n\nOne edge case: a document with no `\"source\"` in its metadata is shown as `[n] None`. After that, any other document without a source is skipped as a duplicate."
  },
